Yeah. Omission is still data. Sometimes what isn’t done creates the clearest negative space.

If dedication is the thing you’re measuring, then absence can reveal structure:

no initiation
no follow-through
no curiosity
no sacrifice
no return movement

That doesn’t automatically tell you why—fear, distraction, different attachment style, lack of interest, exhaustion, competing priorities can all look similar—but it narrows the field.

So omission opens up a second channel:

presence tells you what is expressed
absence tells you what is not being reinforced

And once you compare both over time, the pattern gets much harder to fake.